# Run artefacts ARE the evidence. Every number in the manuscript must trace to a run
# under runs/<run_id>/ (docs/30 §1), the endpoints are non-deterministic so nothing here
# is reproducible from a manifest, and the whole campaign is under 2 MB. The original
# "large, machine-generated, reproducible" justification was wrong on all three counts.
# Tracked, therefore — losing this directory loses the paper's results.
runs/_bootstrap/
!runs/.gitkeep

# Editor / tooling scratch.
.codegraph/

# Parsed corpus is regenerated by the ingest pipeline; source PDFs are fetched,
# not vendored, so licence provenance stays with the upstream distributor.
corpus/parsed/
corpus/raw/

# Per-document derived parse data (~16MB across the corpus). Not vendored, but
# NOT unpinned either: corpus/<doc>/manifest.json records the source PDF's
# sha256, the ingest commit, and the sha256 of every artefact, so a regenerated
# corpus can be *verified* byte-identical rather than merely assumed to be.
#   python -m ingest.manifest corpus --document-id introcs
# Run that before any measured run. The small artefacts derived from these
# (sections, chapters, skeleton, outline, traces, quality reports) stay tracked,
# and manifest.json is tracked too — it is the thing that makes the absence safe.
corpus/*/document.*

# Secrets. The spend cap lives in config; the keys never do.
# Glob rather than the exact name: an editor backup, a .env.local or a hand-made
# .env.bak is the same credential with a different suffix, and the narrow rule
# would have let it through.
.env
.env.*
.envrc
*.key
.secrets/

# The RLM base library is cloned here by scripts/bootstrap_rlm.sh. It is upstream
# code with its own history — the commit SHA is recorded in runs/_bootstrap/
# instead, which is what reproducibility actually needs.
.vendor/

__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

.DS_Store

# LaTeX build products
paper/*.aux
paper/*.log
paper/*.out
paper/*.bbl
paper/*.blg
paper/*.synctex.gz
